WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.897
A controller indication and an independent

2
00:00:03.017 --> 00:00:05.706
reference can serve different purposes.

3
00:00:05.826 --> 00:00:08.930
Define what is being evaluated: sensor
error,

4
00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.484
loop response, stability or spatial
distribution.

5
00:00:12.604 --> 00:00:17.294
A single indication comparison does not
establish vessel uniformity.

6
00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:21.894
Record the reference location, range,
calibration applicability

7
00:00:22.014 --> 00:00:25.531
and the state of the system during the
observation.

8
00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:28.841
Error describes an observed difference;

9
00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:33.444
uncertainty describes doubt associated
with a measurement result.

10
00:00:33.564 --> 00:00:35.564
In this teaching example,

11
00:00:35.684 --> 00:00:42.084
a simple error is +0.4 °C against a
0.5-degree limit.

12
00:00:42.204 --> 00:00:44.962
That comparison alone does not state how

13
00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:47.703
measurement uncertainty is considered.

14
00:00:47.823 --> 00:00:52.237
The protocol needs an appropriate,
predefined decision approach.

15
00:00:52.857 --> 00:00:55.780
Suppose a hypothetical rule accepts only

16
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:03.004
when absolute error plus stated expanded
uncertainty does not exceed 0.5 °C.

17
00:01:03.124 --> 00:01:08.990
With error 0.4 and uncertainty 0.2, the
sum is 0.6.

18
00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.731
This rule would not accept the result.

19
00:01:11.851 --> 00:01:14.610
It is an illustrative conservative rule,

20
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.482
not a universal GMP requirement.

21
00:01:18.102 --> 00:01:23.067
A favorable reading at one instant does
not establish sustained control.

22
00:01:23.187 --> 00:01:25.394
Define the observation interval,

23
00:01:25.514 --> 00:01:28.074
sample timing and evaluation method.

24
00:01:28.194 --> 00:01:30.746
Check for overshoot, drift or cycling

25
00:01:30.866 --> 00:01:33.624
where relevant to the intended function.

26
00:01:33.744 --> 00:01:36.503
Preserve the original trend and context;

27
00:01:36.623 --> 00:01:41.727
selecting only the best-looking interval
can hide an important limitation.
